" (https://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa18053/Download/0018053-15042015121236.pdf)\nciteturn16view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa18053/Download/0018053-15042015121236.pdf\",\"lineno\":375}); Total lines: 420\nL248@P3: assess the quality of the dimensionality reduction at individual\nL249@P3: pixels. This widely varies among different pixels in a single\nL250@P3: image.\nL251@P3: To remedy this issue, we use a pixelwise correlation metric\nL252@P3: as follows:\nL253@P3: γ(i) = XT\nL254@P3: i Yi/|Xi| − X¯iY¯i\nL261@P3: We implemented our method in Microsoft Visual Studio C++\nL262@P3: 2008 with CUDA 4.2 in Windows 7. The hardware included an\nL263@P3: Intel i7-930 CPU clocked at 2.80 GHz, with 12 GB of main\nL264@P3: memory. The graphics processor was an Nvidia GTX 280 with\nL265@P3: a buffer size of 1 GB. We use 12 hyperspectral images that have\nL266@P3: various spectral signatures (see Fig. 2).\nL267@P3: A. Correlation Metric\nL268@P3: We tested the performance of FSPE and compared it against\nL269@P3: other projection methods using correlation metric γ. ISOMAP,\nL270@P3: LLE, and SPE involve free parameters such as the number of\nL271@P3: the neighbors k or neighbor radius rc. For a fair comparison,\nL272@P3: we tried different values of k and rc and only included the best\nL273@P3: result for each of these methods.\nL274@P3: The proposed FSPE is competitive, frequently outperforming\nL275@P3: the other methods. Among the 12 test images, FSPE outper\u0002forms all the other methods with five images and comes as\nL276@P3: the second best performer with three images (see Table I). The\nL277@P3: average performance is 12% better. Note that, since FSPE out\u0002performs PCA, we do not compare against other methods that\nL278@P3: are known to be similar to or outperformed by PCA [16], [17].\nL279@P3: For FSPE, we use \u0003 = 1.0 × 10−8. The initial configuration\nL280@P3: is a random distribution of points in a 3-D cube [0, 1] × [0, 1] ×\nL281@P3: [0, 1]. To avoid oscillatory behavior, learning rate λ(t) linearly\nL282@P3-4: decreases from λ0 = 1.0 to λ1 = 1.0 × 10−3.NAJIM et al.: FSPE: VISUALIZATION OF HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGERY USING FSPE 21\nL283@P4: TABLE I\nL284@P4: PERFORMANCE OF DIMENSIONALITY REDUCTION PROJECTIONS\nL285@P4: MEASURED ACCORDING TO CORRELATION COEFFICIENT γ.\nL286@P4: THE HIGHER, THE BETTER\nL287@P4: Fig. 3. Grayscale images of pixelwise correlation values γ(i). The top row\nL288@P4: shows the color display of a hyperspectral image by PCA, ISOMAP, and FSPE.\nL289@P4: The bottom row presents the corresponding grayscale display of the pixelwise\nL290@P4: correlation values; the brighter, the better (i.e., higher). (a) PCA, γ = 0.691.\nL291@P4: (b) ISOMAP, γ = 0.525. (c) FSPE, γ = 0.863.\nL292@P4: B. Faithful Visualization\nL293@P4: For visualization of hyperspectral images, we use the CIE\nL294@P4: L∗a∗b∗ color space that is perceptually uniform. Among the\nL295@P4: three coordinates in the projection space, we choose the most\nL296@P4: varying coordinate for the L∗ component and the remaining two\nL297@P4: coordinates for the a∗ and b∗ components, followed by a simple\nL298@P4: linear stretching such that L∗ ∈ [0, 100] and that both a∗ and b∗\nL299@P4: have a zero mean.\nL300@P4: Not only providing a quantitative measure of the projection\nL301@P4: quality at each pixel, pixelwise correlation metric γ(i) is also\nL302@P4: useful during the color display and visual examination of hyper\u0002spectral images. In addition to a hyperspectral image in color,\nL303@P4: one may display a supplementary grayscale image. This image\nL304@P4: indicates the reliability of the color at each pixel i by mapping\nL305@P4: γ(i) to a grayscale intensity, i.e., the brighter, the higher (i.e.,\nL306@P4: more reliable) (see Fig. 3). This provides extra information that\nL307@P4: the overall correlation metric γ is unable to provide. Although\nL308@P4: the overall correlation value γ by PCA is higher than that by\nL309@P4: ISOMAP for example, the grayscale display shows that PCA\nL310@P4: yields pixels with γ(i) values that are significantly lower (i.e.,\nL311@P4: darker) than those of the corresponding pixels by ISOMAP (see\nL312@P4: Fig. 3). At these pixels, the quality of dimensionality reduction\nL313@P4: or the reliability of color by PCA is lower than that by ISOMAP.\nL314@P4: Fig. 4. Visualization results of a large hyperspectral image of 1800 ×\nL315@P4: 600 pixels by PCA, SPE, and FSPE.\nL316@P4: Fig. 4 shows the visualization results of a large hyperspectral\nL317@P4: image (1800 × 600 pixels) by PCA, SPE, and FSPE; the\nL318@P4: proposed FSPE outperforms the others.\nL319@P4: C. Local Enhancement\nL320@P4: To demonstrate the local enhancement by FSPE, we choose\nL321@P4: the upper-right part of a hyperspectral image as a region of\nL322@P4: interest; the dimensionality reduction of the image has been\nL323@P4: initially carried out by CDA, which yields a low correlation\nL324@P4: value for the region. Thus, the pixel colors of the region are\nL325@P4: unreliable and even misleading. After the local enhancement\nL326@P4: by FSPE, the correlation value significantly increases, and the\nL327@P4: new colors of the corresponding pixels are more reliable and\nL328@P4: faithful (see Fig. 5).\nL329@P4: D. GPU Implementation\nL330@P4: Nonlinear dimensionality reduction is often too slow to\nL331@P4: compute for interactive visualization. Linear methods, such\nL332@P4: as PCA, are used due to their fast computation [1]. FSPE is\nL333@P4: competitive in computational time. For a hyperspectral image\nL334@P4: of 300 × 300 pixels with 224 spectral bands, the computation\nL335@P4: of FSPE only takes 12.416 s on a CPU; the total number of\nL336@P4: pairwise refinement steps is 9 × 107. Owing to its inherently\nL337@P4: parallel nature, the computation time of FSPE is further reduced\nL338@P4: when benefiting from the parallel computing power of low\u0002cost GPUs. The computation of FSPE takes 0.011 s on a GPU,\nL339@P4: yielding a speed up of two orders of magnitude and making it\nL340@P4-5: suitable for interactive visualization.22 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL341@P5: Fig. 5. Local enhancement by FSPE. (a) Color display of a hyperspectral\nL342@P5: image by CDA and its corresponding grayscale image of pixelwise correlation\nL343@P5: γ(i). The upper-right part of it is chosen as a region of interest. Since the corre\u0002lation value of the region is low (γ = 0.036), its colors appearing (relatively)\nL344@P5: homogeneous may not be reliable and misleadingly implies the homogeneity\nL345@P5: of spectral signatures. (b) After the local enhancement by FSPE, the correlation\nL346@P5: value of the region significantly increases to γ = 0.890; thus, its colors are now\nL347@P5: more reliable, showing more heterogeneity of spectral signatures.\nL348@P5: Fig. 6. Computation time of the FSPE algorithm for hyperspectral images\nL349@P5: with different sizes. The GPU implementation maintains a speed up of two\nL350@P5: orders of magnitude compared with the CPU implementation.\nL351@P5: As other nonlinear projection methods often involve the\nL352@P5: eigendecomposition or computation of geodesic distances, they\nL353@P5: are more challenging to speed up their computation by GPUs.\nL354@P5: Fig. 6 shows the running time of the GPU implementation\nL355@P5: of FSPE compared with the CPU implementation for differ\u0002ent data sizes, i.e., 1 × 300 × 300, 2 × 300 × 300,..., 12 ×\nL356@P5: 300 × 300 pixels; for a hyperspectral image of a million pixels,\nL357@P5: the implementation of FSPE on a GPU takes less than 16 s.\nL358@P5: VI. CONCLUSION\nL359@P5: FSPE is a novel nonlinear dimensionality reduction method\nL360@P5: for visualizing hyperspectral imagery. While considering the\nL361@P5: nonlinear characteristics of data points in the high-dimensional\nL362@P5: spectral space, FSPE avoids the costly computation of explicitly\nL363@P5: estimating geodesic distances that are often required by other\nL364@P5: nonlinear methods. The experimental results prove that FSPE is\nL365@P5: competitive with or outperforms state-of-art methods in hyper\u0002spectral image visualization. We also demonstrate that, due to\nL366@P5: its parallel nature, FSPE takes advantage of parallel computing\nL367@P5: devices such as GPUs, leading to substantial speed ups and\nL368@P5: interactive applications. We also utilize a pixelwise metric that\nL369@P5: measures the quality of hyperspectral image visualization at\nL370@P5: individual pixels. The future work includes an optimized GPU\nL371@P5: implementation of FSPE aiming at processing a hyperspectral\nL372@P5: image of a million pixels within a second or so and improving\nL373@P5: the pixelwise metric with a weighted version. The source code\nL374@P5: of FSPE is available [18].\nL375@P5: REFERENCES\nL376@P5: [1] M. Cui, A. Razdan, J. Hu, and P. Wonka, “Interactive hyperspectral image\nL377@P5: visualization using convex optimization,” IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote\nL378@P5: Sens., vol. 47, no. 6, pp. 1673–1684, Jun. 2009.\nL379@P5: [2] J. S. Tyo, A. Konsolakis, D. I. Diersen, and R. C. Olsen, “Principal\u0002components-based display strategy for spectral imagery,” IEEE Trans.\nL380@P5: Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 41, no. 3, pp. 708–718, Mar. 2003.\nL381@P5: [3] C. M. Bachmann, T. L. Ainsworth, and R. A. Fusina, “Improved mani\u0002fold coordinate representations of large-scale hyperspectral scenes,” IEEE\nL382@P5: Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 44, no. 10, pp. 2786–2803, Oct. 2006.\nL383@P5: [4] J. B. Tenenbaum, V. de Silva, and J. C. Langford, “A global geometric\nL384@P5: framework for nonlinear dimensionality reduction,” Science, vol. 290,\nL385@P5: no. 5500, pp. 2319–2323, Dec. 2000.\nL386@P5: [5] A. Mohan, G. Sapiro, and E. Bosch, “Spatially coherent nonlinear di\u0002mensionality reduction and segmentation of hyperspectral images,” IEEE\nL387@P5: Geosci. Remote Sens. Lett., vol. 4, no. 2, pp. 206–210, Apr. 2007.\nL388@P5: [6] S. Roweis and L. Saul, “Nonlinear dimensionality reduction by locally lin\u0002ear embedding,” Science, vol. 290, no. 5500, pp. 2323–2326, Dec. 2000.\nL389@P5: [7] Q. Du, N. Raksuntorn, S. Cai, and R. Moorhead, “Color display for\nL390@P5: hyperspectral imagery,” IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 46, no. 6,\nL391@P5: pp. 1858–1866, Jun. 2008.\nL392@P5: [8] M. Radovanovic, A. Nanopoulos, and M. Ivanovi ´ c, “Hubs in space: Pop- ´\nL393@P5: ular nearest neighbors in high-dimensional data,” J. Mach. Learning Res.,\nL394@P5: vol. 11, pp. 2487–2531, Sep. 2010.\nL395@P5: [9] N. Jacobson and M. Gupta, “Design goals and solutions for display of\nL396@P5: hyperspectral images,” IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 43, no. 11,\nL397@P5: pp. 2684–2692, Nov. 2005.\nL398@P5: [10] S. France and J. Carroll, “Two-way multidimensional scaling: A review,”\nL399@P5: IEEE Trans. Syst., Man, Cybern., Part C: Appl. Rev., vol. 41, no. 5,\nL400@P5: pp. 644–661, Sep. 2011.\nL401@P5: [11] T. F. Cox and M. A. A. Cox, Multidimensional Scaling, 2nd ed. Boca\nL402@P5: Raton, FL, USA: CRC Press, 2000.\nL403@P5: [12] P. Demartines and J. Herault, “Curvilinear component analysis: A self\u0002organizing neural network for nonlinear mapping of data sets,” IEEE\nL404@P5: Trans. Neural Netw., vol. 8, no. 1, pp. 148–154, Jan. 1997.\nL405@P5: [13] J. A. Lee, A. Lendasse, and M. Verleysen, “Nonlinear projection\nL406@P5: with curvilinear distances: Isomap versus curvilinear distance analysis,”\nL407@P5: Neurocomputing, vol. 57, pp. 49–76, Mar. 2004.\nL408@P5: [14] V. de Silva and J. B. Tenenbaum, “Local versus global methods for\nL409@P5: nonlinear dimensionality reduction,” in Advances in Neural Information\nL410@P5: Processing Systems, vol. 15. Cambridge, MA, USA: MIT Press, 2003,\nL411@P5: pp. 705–712.\nL412@P5: [15] D. K. Agrafiotis and H. Xu, “A self-organizing principle for learning non\u0002linear manifolds,” Proc. Nat. Acad. Sci. U.S.A., vol. 99, no. 25, pp. 15869–\nL413@P5: 15872, Dec. 2002.\nL414@P5: [16] M. Mignotte, “A bicriteria-optimization-approach-based dimensionality\u0002reduction model for the color display of hyperspectral images,” IEEE\nL415@P5: Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 50, no. 2, pp. 501–513, Feb. 2012.\nL416@P5: [17] Z. Mahmood and P. Scheunders, “Enhanced visualization of hyperspectral\nL417@P5: images,” IEEE Geosci. Remote Sens. Lett., vol. 8, no. 5, pp. 869–873,\nL418@P5: Sep. 2011.\nL419@P5: [18] [Online]. Available: https://sites.google.com/site/fspedr/\n"
